{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 10066 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7f05d375d1d66588e6a2826efcb673aea4830df61a2209dc79355d16d95031a4" :edb/db-before-t 10065 :edb/db-after-t 10066 :edb/tx-data [[13194139543378 50 #inst "2026-10-28T18:10:54.917Z" 13194139543378 17592186576411 true] [17592186365398 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139543378 17592186576411 false] [17592186365398 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$\n\nUse the free parameter directly as its corresponding Cartesian coordinate." 13194139543378 17592186576411 true] [17592186365398 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$" 13194139543378 17592186576411 false] [17592186365398 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$\n\nHere the free parameter is $z$. The stated direct-coordinate convention therefore requires the third component to be $z$." 13194139543378 17592186576411 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
